course: dictionary
lesson: 2331
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant. Crucify most often means to kill someone by nailing or tying them to a cross, a cruel punishment used in ancient times, especially by the Romans. «The Romans crucified rebels and slaves.» The clues are the Romans and the ancient past, so here it means that punishment.
explain: «My mum will crucify me if I'm late!» is a different meaning. Nobody is nailed to anything. It is an exaggeration, and it means she will be very angry with you. Always ask: does this sentence make literal sense, or is it a figure of speech?
explain: In religious education, crucify is used of Jesus. Christians believe he was crucified and rose again, and they remember his death on Good Friday and his rising at Easter. Sport and newspapers use it in other ways too, such as «The newspapers crucified the manager», which means they criticised him very harshly.
fact: Crucify means to kill someone by nailing or tying them to a cross, as a cruel punishment in ancient times.
fact: The Romans used crucifixion as a punishment, and they crucified rebels and slaves.
fact: In an informal way, «crucify» can mean to criticise someone very harshly, or to beat someone very heavily in sport.
fact: Good Friday remembers the death of Jesus, and Easter remembers his rising.
fact: When a word has several meanings, read the sentence around it to find the right one.
exercise: «The Romans crucified rebels and slaves.» What does crucified mean here? | *To punish someone cruelly by nailing or tying them to a cross | To paint a picture of a cross | To hide something in a box | To hug someone warmly
exercise: «My mum will crucify me if I'm late!» What does crucify mean in this sentence? | To nail someone to a wooden cross | To run very fast to school | To be late for school | *To be very angry with someone, as an exaggeration
exercise: «Christians believe Jesus was crucified and rose again on the third day.» Which festival remembers the death of Jesus? | Christmas | *Good Friday | Diwali | Bonfire Night
test: «Our team was crucified by the other side, losing 7–0.» What does crucified mean? | Asked to sit down | Nailed to a wooden board | *Beaten very heavily | Given a medal
test: «The newspapers crucified the manager.» Which meaning fits best? | Kept safe in a museum | Hung on a cross in a picture | Given a gift | *Criticised very harshly
exam: Jayden reads «Jesus was crucified» in a history book about ancient times, then sees «crucified» in a sports report. Which meaning fits the history book, and which clue shows it? | Ran across a field, because of the word crossed | *Killed on a cross, because the book is about ancient punishment | Painted in bright colours, because history is colourful | Crossed out of a list, because of the word crossed
